WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.586
Pitcairn's most famous history begins with a mutiny, but people lived here centuries before.

00:00:06.686 --> 00:00:10.567
Polynesians lived on Pitcairn and Henderson for several centuries.

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:14.212
They left house sites, burials, rock carvings and stone tools.

00:00:14.212 --> 00:00:18.739
Pitcairn's fine-grained basalt and volcanic glass were traded with Mangareva,

00:00:18.739 --> 00:00:21.209
about five hundred kilometres to the west.

00:00:21.209 --> 00:00:25.207
When that exchange collapsed, these marginal islands were abandoned.

00:00:25.207 --> 00:00:26.795
Europeans found them empty.

00:00:27.895 --> 00:00:33.555
In April 1789 the crew of HMS Bounty mutinied against their captain, William Bligh.

00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:38.056
Fletcher Christian took eight other mutineers, six Polynesian men,

00:00:38.056 --> 00:00:41.739
about a dozen Polynesian women and a baby to Pitcairn,

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:44.671
where they burned the ship in January 1790.

00:00:45.771 --> 00:00:51.524
Pitcairn had been charted with the wrong longitude, which made it a perfect hiding place.

00:00:51.524 --> 00:00:53.916
Violence soon killed most of the men.

00:00:53.916 --> 00:00:59.152
When an American ship found the community in 1808, only one mutineer, John Adams,

00:00:59.152 --> 00:01:00.187
was still alive.

00:01:00.187 --> 00:01:05.746
In 1856 the entire population of one hundred and ninety-three moved to Norfolk Island;

00:01:05.746 --> 00:01:07.621
some families later returned.

00:01:08.721 --> 00:01:13.928
In 1814 two British warships, Briton and Tagus, called at the island.

00:01:13.928 --> 00:01:16.569
An officer on board drew this view.

00:01:17.669 --> 00:01:22.494
In 1825 Captain Frederick Beechey of HMS Blossom surveyed the island.

00:01:22.494 --> 00:01:26.690
His Admiralty chart finally put Pitcairn in the right place.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.637
Pitcairn has remained British.

00:01:29.637 --> 00:01:34.562
In 2004 a series of trials for historic sexual offences led to the conviction of

00:01:34.562 --> 00:01:35.732
several island men.

00:01:35.732 --> 00:01:37.640
The case divided the community,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.827
and raised hard questions about law and governance on a tiny island.
